WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.439
Hey guys!

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:06.200
Right before the pandemic struck, Julia and
I made a very special episode we're excited

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.970
to finally share with you.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:13.160
We visited a financial therapist to discuss
a challenge we've struggled with for years.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:18.311
Now if we did this today, we'd probably do
it outdoors with masks, and have a lot of

00:00:18.311 --> 00:00:19.890
different issues to talk about.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:25.290
But we still think it's relevant to consider
how your emotional history impacts your financial

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.290
decision-making.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.550
Spoiler alert: I get a little weepy.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.900
Julia and I have worked professionally as
financial advisors for many years.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.410
Over that time, we've helped a lot of clients,
expanded our knowledge base, and started a

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.670
popular YouTube show.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.260
But one thing we've never done is raise our
fees.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.940
That's right, nervous newbie Julia and Philip
charged the same rates as experienced, confident

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:46.999
Julia and Philip.

00:00:46.999 --> 00:00:50.140
We've talked about it for a long time but
it just feels so weird.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.890
Like, I think we're worth it, but for some
reason, it's really scary to pull that trigger.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:59.710
So on this special episode of Two Cents, we've
decided to turn the tables and get help from

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:00.710
someone else.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.800
Oh, and you can lose the background, we won't
be needing that.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.590
We're here at the office of Michelle Bohls,
a therapist who specializes in the emotions

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.480
that money can cause in people's lives.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.110
I'll admit it, I'm a little nervous.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.610
I mean, I'd like to think that we're pretty
financially savvy, but you never know what

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.210
can come up once you start digging.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.650
Just remember, whatever happens is between
you, me and the therapist.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:30.650
Right.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.200
And our hundreds of thousands of subscribers.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.290
Good point.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.800
Let's go inside.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.480
Hi!

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:42.300
I'm Michelle!

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.440
I'm Julia.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.600
Thank you so much for having us here today,
Michelle.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.460
We are excited and nervous.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.710
So, what is it you actually do?

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:59.840
Well, so, I'm a financial therapist, specifically
helping couples work with money in their relationship.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.370
And a financial therapist is not a financial
person that's gonna sit down with your bills

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.700
or your budget or tell you what to do with
your money.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:13.500
But really looking at the belief systems that
you're bringing in about the money, and why

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.459
you as a couple might get stuck on particular
decisions.

00:02:18.459 --> 00:02:21.909
My dad was a CPA, my parents were both very
poor.

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:26.370
I just naturally gravitated towards understanding
money.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:31.280
But then, seeing couples--and money's such
a typical issue for couples.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:38.299
And it can be hard to talk about, because
there's this idea of being not a polite topic

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:43.459
in lower socioeconomic circles or middle class.

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:50.599
But the upper class, the 1%, will talk about
money quite comfortably and with their children.

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:53.530
And they'll educate them about money.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:03:00.410
And so, a lot of us come out into the world
without the information we need about money.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:04.209
Okay, so, Michelle, what should we expect
as we go into this session?

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:08.230
We've never been in a financial therapy session
before.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:12.430
Um, I can tell you I don't know what to expect,
because I've never been in a therapy session

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:13.720
with the two of you before.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.480
So we're all kind of in it together.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.480
Okay, we're learning as we go.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.230
And all I can tell you is, we all get stuck.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.600
And it's really just, do you have a few tools
to get unstuck.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:23.660
We're excited.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:24.500
Yeah, I think we're ready.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.969
Okay, great, well let's start then.

00:03:27.969 --> 00:03:32.120
Money gets to the heart of our sense of safety.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.669
It's often associated with different types
of threats.

00:03:35.669 --> 00:03:42.279
It's either threatening to make it, it's threatening
to spend it or it's threatening to save it.

00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:47.060
And there's all kinds of things that we do
avoid feeling those fears that are connected

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.060
to money.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:54.180
So, when I think about raising our fee, I
worry like we'll just be for rich people.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.439
And I don't wanna be.

00:03:57.439 --> 00:04:03.060
You worry that by raising our fees, we won't
be able to help as many people.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.629
Is that right?

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:05.629
Yes.

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:12.200
Just see if you can remember anything from
your history where you started to get the

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:19.099
idea that some people have money and some
people don't have money, and that something

00:04:19.099 --> 00:04:21.599
is nefarious about it.

00:04:21.599 --> 00:04:22.599
Yeah.

00:04:22.599 --> 00:04:29.699
I think... this is kinda random why I think
of this, but I remember growing up, my dad,

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:33.050
he was a used car salesman.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:41.090
He one year won a trip to Hawaii because he
was such a great salesperson.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:43.710
And his boss took it from him.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:51.590
The owner of this big car dealership who had
all the money that he could've possibly wanted,

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:56.110
he took that vacation from my dad.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:03.740
So when I think about that man, the story
I make up about him is...

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:23.759
I think, the story that I make up about that
man is that his wealth made him blind to the

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.840
needs of others.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.930
I remember how mad my mother was.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:36.340
My mom was-- she wasn't mad about the loss
of this trip.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.460
She was mad about how poorly he'd been treated.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:44.259
When we see that other people have money or
don't have money, that also generates a lot

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:45.990
of feelings in us.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:49.370
We can feel greedy, like we need more.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.300
We can feel deprived, like we don't have enough.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:56.319
We can feel ashamed of what we have.

00:05:56.319 --> 00:06:00.349
We can feel envy for what they have.

00:06:00.349 --> 00:06:05.449
So really money is a very emotional topic
for all of us.

00:06:05.449 --> 00:06:10.520
When I think about raising our fees, I have
a couple feelings.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:17.280
One of them is... um... a feeling of being
corrupt.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.939
I mean, I'm really worried of what people
will think of us.

00:06:23.939 --> 00:06:26.800
Even talking about it, not even doing it.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.080
Maybe, are you worried about, like, our fans
and our subscribers thinking...

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.610
Yeah.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.409
And the story I make up is that they will
think...

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:44.200
The story I make up is they will think we
are... predatory and frauds.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:52.620
I definitely started to feel some changes
in how I was thinking about ideas of integrity

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.729
and corruption.

00:06:53.729 --> 00:06:58.300
Saying how we feel about this together with
somebody else in the room, just kinda made

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:01.680
it less intimidating, less shameful.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.060
When I first became a financial advisor, my
first job, I saw advisors who were older than

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:16.360
me doing things and selling things that, to
my mind, looked like they only helped out

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.920
the advisor and didn't help out the person
on the other end.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:22.479
And do you now think that this is equivalent
to doing that?

00:07:22.479 --> 00:07:23.500
Mm hm.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:24.690
Or could be.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.720
Could be. Could be. Good.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.020
All of our stories come from our history.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.300
That we really can't come from anywhere but
our own experiences.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:39.729
I mean, we have an imagination, but when it
comes to emotional things, it's usually from

00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:41.879
our memory, our memory networks.

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:46.160
The stories that our minds make up, we call
those projections.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:52.050
And when your past creates projections, that
blocks connection for the couple.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:58.749
When I was younger, I was really into literature,
and loved to read the classics.

00:07:58.749 --> 00:08:04.560
And in so many stories, the evil, corrupt
character is the wealthy one.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.250
It was part of the reason I decided to become
an artist and study art in school.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.099
That seemed like the exact opposite direction.

00:08:12.099 --> 00:08:19.430
I knew I'd be poor but true to myself, if
I ran away from a lucrative career.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:22.699
It's a very primitive fear.

00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:28.030
If we're all living in a village, right? And
I'm hoarding food and water while you're starving...

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:29.780
You're gonna come kill me.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.320
And that's really--that was the correct response.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.339
That's how some of our ancestors got here.

00:08:35.339 --> 00:08:43.620
So, understanding that when we have more,
there is a threat level, you know, to having

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:46.110
more when other people don't have.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:52.339
But especially if we saw anyone get hurt by
that dynamic as a child.

00:08:52.339 --> 00:08:56.860
As an adult, when I look at that decision...

00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:00.610
I have a hard time trusting myself.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:01.950
I trust you.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:05.880
I trust you more than I trust myself.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:11.570
When I see you being decisive, I feel more
confident.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:16.500
What comes up for me hearing you say that?

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:24.260
When I hear you say that, um, it worries me.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:30.970
I get a little anxious about whether I am
making right decisions.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.700
So in those moments what I might need from
you...

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:40.900
In those moments, what I might need from you
is to hear

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:44.900
that it'll be okay if I don't make the best choice sometimes.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:52.300
That if I make a mistake, that you can still
trust me.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:56.720
I actually did find it quite surprising that
we were looking at each other, that she was

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:02.949
not giving us advice to do this or don't do
this, because that's what an advisor does.

00:10:02.949 --> 00:10:10.420
I would say she created a safe emotional place
to talk about non-safe ideas.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:14.610
Working with Julia and Philip in that session,
I'm noticing, okay, there's like a lot of

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:15.670
strengths here.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:19.480
They're able to listen to each other, be curious.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:25.110
Whichever way they decide to go, I know that
their relationship's better for having learned

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:26.930
where some of the energy was coming from.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:27.930
Yay!

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:28.930
Thank you!

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:29.930
Thank you very very much.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:30.930
Thank you, Michelle.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:35.480
Because of our work, Philip and I are already
pretty used to discussing money issues openly

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:39.910
with each other, but most couples struggle
to get on the same financial page.

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:43.850
You might disagree with your partner about
spending priorities, get frustrated with their

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:49.540
tendency to save too much or not enough, or
harbor resentment about different income levels.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:54.100
Finance and therapy are two things that have
unfortunate stigmas that might discourage

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:57.660
people from getting the help that could alleviate
their problems.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:02.980
We hope that watching us go through this will
make it seem a little less intimidating for you.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:05.240
And that's our two cents!

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:09.320
Thanks to our patrons for keeping Two Cents financially healthy!

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:13.200
Click the link in the description to become a Two Cents patron.

